    <p>
      <strong>Proof of Proposition <a href="/tag/1FDDC5" data-tag="1FDDC5">5.4.7.4</a>.</strong>
       Let $K$ be a $\kappa $-small simplicial set and let $p: K \rightarrow \calC ^{\tau }$ be a diagram which admits a limit $X \in \calC $. We wish to show that $X$ is $\tau $-compact. According to Lemma <a href="/tag/DBC388" data-tag="DBC388">5.4.7.5</a>, it suffices to prove that the space $F(X)$ is essentially $\tau $-small, where $F: \calC \rightarrow \SSet $ denotes the functor co-represented by a $\kappa $-compact object $C \in \calC $. Since $F$ preserves limits, we note that $F(X)$ is a limit of $F \circ p$. Lemma <a href="/tag/DBC388" data-tag="DBC388">5.4.7.5</a> implies that the diagram $F \circ p$ takes values in $\SSet ^{\tau } \subseteq \SSet $. We now conclude by applying Lemma <a href="/tag/34B68F" data-tag="34B68F">5.4.7.6</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
